Package: oneplus-erhai-torch
Version: 0.1-test-01
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Architecture: all
Maintainer: TheMojoMan <themojoman@gmx.net>
Depends: udev
Recommends: gnome-shell
Description: Torch (rear camera flash LED) control for the OnePlus Pad 3 (erhai)
 Turns the rear camera flash into a usable torch with on/off and a
 brightness slider, driven from a GNOME Quick Settings quick slider
 (erhai-torch@erhai.local).
 .
 The rear flash is the pm8550 SPMI flash-LED controller (led-controller@ee00)
 exposed by leds-qcom-flash as two LEDs, white:flash-0 (channels 1+4) and
 white:flash-1 (channels 2+3). Writing the LED class 'brightness' attribute
 puts the LED into TORCH mode: the driver scales the torch current against
 led-max-microamp (500 mA per LED, split over the two channels), and a value
 of 0 disables the module again. That is exactly what a torch slider wants,
 so the extension drives 'brightness' directly.
 .
 Writing a sysfs attribute is a privileged operation, and 'uaccess' does not
 help here: LED class devices have no device node, so logind has nothing to
 put an ACL on. The shipped udev rule (71-erhai-torch.rules) instead chgrp's
 the LED attributes to a dedicated 'erhai-torch' group and makes them group
 writable at device-add time, and the postinst puts the desktop users into
 that group. The slider therefore writes the LED itself, with no root helper,
 no polkit prompt and no round trip through a service.
 .
 Group membership takes effect at the next login, so an existing session may
 need one logout before the slider can drive the LED; the extension reports
 exactly that if the write is refused.
 .
 The extension switches the torch off when it is disabled (including at
 logout), so a panel toggle can never leave the flash burning unattended.
